<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <!-- viewport-fit=cover: draw under notches/rounded corners in the installed
         app; user-scalable=no: pinch-zoom fights the game's own input. The soft
         keyboard is handled in-game by hud.ts's lift: while one of the game's
         chat boxes is focused it adds interactive-widget=overlays-content to
         THIS tag (and virtualKeyboard.overlaysContent where the API exists), so
         the keys OVERLAY the world+HUD without scrolling or resizing them, and
         restores the content below when the keys are gone — the select screen's
         name field, the wiki drawer and the drop dialog keep the browser's
         default. Do NOT add interactive-widget=resizes-visual here: it makes the
         browser scroll the whole game up to reveal the focused chat box
         (maintainer). -->
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0, viewport-fit=cover, user-scalable=no" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#000000" />
    <title>Nangijala</title>
    <!-- PWA: installable on the Android home screen; starts fullscreen. -->
    <link rel="manifest" href="/manifest.webmanifest" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/icons/icon-192.png" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/icons/apple-touch-icon.png" />
    <meta name="mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes" />
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes" />
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-status-bar-style" content="black-translucent" />
    <style>
      /* touch-action pan-x pan-y: panning (list scrolling) stays allowed but
         PINCH-ZOOM and double-tap-zoom are blocked — crucially this works
         even under "Desktop site", where the viewport meta (incl.
         user-scalable=no) is ignored. main.ts adds the JS belt-and-braces. */
      html, body { margin: 0; height: 100%; background: #000; overflow: hidden;
        overscroll-behavior: none; touch-action: pan-x pan-y; }
      /* This is a game, not a document: no text selection anywhere (a long
         press on Android selected UI text — e.g. the update toast's version
         hash — and popped Chrome's "search results" sheet over the game).
         Inputs keep normal text editing. */
      body { user-select: none; -webkit-user-select: none; -webkit-touch-callout: none;
        -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent; }
      input, textarea { user-select: text; -webkit-user-select: text; }
      /* THE CAMERA BAND IS PAINTED, SO THE GAME LOOKS THE SAME EVERY LAUNCH
         (maintainer 2026-09-13: "the players HP/EP/XP-bar/card is not at the
         top … I then restarted the game and that fixed the issue", then the
         trigger, from him: "It happens when I tab out and back in again!").
         His shell letterboxes the camera cutout, and on a resume from the task
         switcher it hands the app the whole screen instead — so the cutout
         state flips mid-session, not just per launch. MEASURED on his two shots: the card does not move — its HP
         bar is 198 vs 196 device px from the top of the GLASS — because the
         chips clear the cutout through --ml-safe-top (theme.ts). What changes
         is only what fills the 152 device px (55.3 css px) above it: the black
         letterbox, or the live world. World up there reads as "the card is
         floating", which is the report. So the band wears the same #000 the
         shell's letterbox does — it is the phone's bezel either way — and the
         two launches are pixel-alike. NOT the alternative (chips flush to the
         glass at 10px): that is the layout the cutout inset exists to prevent,
         and a top-corner camera hole then sits on the HP numbers.
         Paint only, NO layout: nothing is inset, the canvas is not re-fitted,
         and the golden split is untouched — the world still renders behind the
         band, it is simply covered, exactly as the letterbox covers it.
         Zero height (so zero paint) wherever there is no cutout or the shell
         letterboxes it. LANDSCAPE is unaffected: the cutout is a left/right
         inset there and the top one collapses. z 9 = over the world, the
         rotation veil (3) and the chips (8); under the update toast (100), the
         loading overlay (20) and the rotate prompt (1000), all of which are
         full-screen chrome that should cover it. pointer-events:none so a tap
         in the band still reaches the world. Gate: verify-safearea section 4. */
      #ml-safebar { position: fixed; top: 0; left: 0; right: 0; z-index: 9;
        height: env(safe-area-inset-top, 0px); background: #000;
        pointer-events: none; }
      /* dvh tracks the REAL visible height on mobile (address bar shown/hidden);
         vh is the fallback for older browsers. touch-action here (NOT on body:
         the select screen's lists still need touch scrolling) stops double-tap
         zoom / pan gestures from fighting the game's tap-to-move.
         GOLDEN-RATIO split: the game viewport is the top 61.8%, the bottom
         38.2% is the HUD (client/src/hud.ts — framed tab row + content page;
         backpack/inventory to come). */
      :root { --hud-h: 38.2vh; --hud-h-inv: 61.8vh; }
      @supports (height: 1dvh) { :root { --hud-h: 38.2dvh; --hud-h-inv: 61.8dvh; } }
      /* THE CANVAS KEEPS THE THREE-ROW SPLIT WHEN A SUB-TAB STRIP IS OPEN:
         --hud-h-inv is the HUD's real top edge (it rises by the strip when
         Settings opens, and everything anchored above the rail rides with
         it), but the game view adds --sub-h back — a canvas resize is a
         framebuffer realloc plus a whole-world redraw, and the strip's slide
         would fire one per frame. The world under the strip is covered, not
         resized (hud.ts applyLayout, 2026-09-19). 0 whenever no strip is open
         and in landscape, so this is the plain split everywhere else. */
      #game { width: 100vw; height: calc(var(--hud-h-inv) + var(--sub-h, 0px)); touch-action: none; }
      /* IN-GAME LANDSCAPE (hud.ts applyLayout sets .ml-land + the px vars):
         the split turns on its side — the menu is a --menu-w side column and
         the game view takes the rest at full height (--hud-h-inv is published
         as the full innerHeight then). --gv-left is the menu's width when the
         menu sits on the LEFT (right-handed, the default) and 0 when it sits
         on the right (left-handed), so this one margin places the view for
         both. The canvas follows #game via main.ts's ResizeObserver. */
      html.ml-land #game { width: calc(100vw - var(--menu-w, 38.2vw));
        margin-left: var(--gv-left, 0px); }
      canvas { display: block; image-rendering: pixelated; }
      /* Portrait-only OUTSIDE the world: on the title/select/loading screens,
         landscape on a small touch screen still gets the "rotate back"
         prompt. Once in the world (hud.ts sets html.ml-ingame) the prompt
         stays hidden — landscape is a real layout there since 2026-08-05.
         Desktops/laptops (fine pointer) and tall tablets are unaffected. */
      #ml-rotate { display: none; }
      @media (orientation: landscape) and (pointer: coarse) and (max-height: 520px) {
        #ml-rotate { display: flex; position: fixed; inset: 0; z-index: 1000;
          align-items: center; justify-content: center; text-align: center;
          background: #000; color: #e8e8f0; font: 600 20px system-ui, sans-serif; }
        #ml-rotate small { display: block; margin-top: 8px; font: 400 13px system-ui, sans-serif; color: #9aa0bf; }
      }
      html.ml-ingame #ml-rotate { display: none !important; }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div id="ml-safebar"></div>
    <div id="game"></div>
    <div id="ml-rotate"><div>📱<br />Rotate your phone<small>The menus play upright — landscape unlocks once you enter the world</small></div></div>
    <script type="module" src="/assets/index-143cb682da.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>
